{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# The completion gate cannot verify a large epic: step budget exhausts at 480 AND at 1600\n\n> **Scope note.** The title states the reported end-state. THIS ticket delivers only the\n> `search_files` file-path fix — the loop's fuel and an independent signed-gate false-FAIL vector.\n> Full epic-verifiability is restored jointly with the four sibling tickets linked\n> `discovered_from` this one (loop breaker, recovery reachability, diagnostic sidecar,\n> `--force-close` remedy).\n\n## The failure\n\nClosing epic [rebar:e369-a449-4773-48fb] (59 descendants, all closed and certified) fails:\n\n    Error: cannot close e369-a449-4773-48fb: completion verification could not run\n    (completion verification workflow did not produce a verdict: step 'decide@then/verify' failed:\n     agent exceeded its step budget (max_iterations=480; ~1 model request per tool call).\n     Raise REBAR_LLM_MAX_STEPS or narrow the task.)\n\nRetried with `REBAR_LLM_MAX_STEPS=1600` (3.3x the default). **Exhausted again**, after ~50 minutes,\nidentical message, `max_iterations=1600`.\n\n## MEASURED root cause (rebar-debug Phase 1 — see the RCA comment)\n\nAn instrumented reproduction (every agent tool call logged; 264 calls, 1074s, `max_iterations=480`)\nsettled the question by measurement: **the verifier LOOPS; it does not converge at any budget.**\nNovelty by quartile: calls 1-132 are 94-98% novel, calls 199-264 are **0% novel**. From call #121\n(t=537s) the agent is in a closed 4-call cycle repeated 31-37x against a single file.\n\nThe loop's engine is a tool defect: **`search_files` silently returns `\"(no matches)\"` when its\n`path` argument names a FILE rather than a directory** (and likewise for a path that does not\nexist). `src/rebar/llm/pai_tools.py:109` walks `os.walk(base)`; `os.walk` on a file yields nothing,\nso control falls through to `return \"\\n\".join(hits) or \"(no matches)\"` at `pai_tools.py:141`. There\nis no file/directory discrimination and no existence check.\n\nGround-truthed live in-run false negatives: `search_files(\"J1\", \".../test_backend_characterization.py\")`\n-> `(no matches)` while `J1` is on line 116 of that file (this is the loop); `allow_insecure` in\n`src/rebar/_config_schema.py` -> `(no matches)` with **21 real hits**; `resolved_statuses` in the same\nfile -> `(no matches)` with 5 real hits. Aggregate: **48% of `search_files` calls passed a file path,\n100% of those returned `(no matches)`, and 26 of 29 had the literal genuinely present** — 29% of every\ntool call in the run was a guaranteed-false negative.\n\nBeyond the wasted budget this is a **false-FAIL vector on a signed gate**: the packaged\n`completion-verifier` prompt tells the agent that `(no matches)` means \"only that *that literal\nstring* is absent\", and encourages narrowing the search path — the exact move that returns a lie.\n\n## Scope of THIS ticket\n\nThis ticket fixes **only** the `search_files` path-handling defect. It is the smallest change that\nremoves the loop's fuel and closes the signed-gate false-FAIL vector. Four further defects were\nproven in the same investigation and are filed separately (linked `discovered_from` this ticket)\nbecause each changes gate control flow or operator messaging and deserves its own review:\n\n* no loop breaker — `RunRequest.tool_step_limit` is never set for the primary verify step;\n* the bounded completion recovery is unreachable for a step-budget `LLMRunnerError`;\n* the repetition diagnostic is computed and then dropped (no `gate_error_v1` sidecar);\n* the close gate offers `--force-close` as the remedy for a RESOURCE exhaustion.\n\n`read_file`'s inverted-range fallback (`pai_tools.py:63`) is also deliberately **out of scope**: it\nwas not part of the proven mechanism (the inverted read actually returned text containing the sought\nliteral), and changing a second function's contract in this repo-wide shared module would materially\nwiden the review surface. Filed separately.\n\n## The fix\n\nIn `src/rebar/llm/pai_tools.py`, `search_files` must resolve its `path` argument before walking:\n\n* `path` names an existing **file** -> scan that single file and return its `file:line: text` hits,\n  applying the `_SCAN_MAX_FILES` / `_SEARCH_MAX_HITS` caps. It must **not** apply the DISCOVERY\n  filter: `skip_file` (`fs_tools.py:124-127`) hides gitignored files via `_git_tracked`\n  (`fs_tools.py:95-113`), and applying it to an explicitly named path would answer `\"(no matches)\"`\n  for a file that plainly contains the literal — this bug's lie in different clothes. `read_file`\n  already settled the precedent (`fs_tools.py:42-43`): \"an explicitly named file is always readable\n  (only the security deny-list blocks it)\";\n* `path` names an existing **directory** -> **unchanged** `os.walk` behaviour (every existing caller\n  depends on this; the contract must not move);\n* `path` does not exist -> return an explicit `Error: ...` string, never `\"(no matches)\"`, so an\n  agent can tell \"I mistyped the path\" from \"the literal is absent\".\n\n`\"(no matches)\"` must from now on mean exactly one thing: the literal was searched for and is absent.\n\n`search_files`'s **docstring must be updated in the same change**. Pydantic AI binds plain functions\nand reads their signature + docstring as the model-facing tool description (`pai_tools.py:46`), so the\ndocstring IS the contract the agent is given. Today it says only \"search across text files under\n`path`\". Fixing the behaviour without the docstring would leave agents still believing a file path is\nnot a valid `path`, which defeats the point of the fix — the looping verifier narrowed to a file path\nprecisely because the packaged prompt told it to.\n\n## Blast radius\n\n`pai_tools.py` supplies the read-only tools to **every** LLM gate (plan-review, code-review,\ncompletion), so the change is repo-wide in effect even though the diff is one function. The\ndirectory-path branch is therefore held byte-identical in behaviour and is pinned by a regression\ntest, and the change is additive: it converts two cases that currently return a false\n`\"(no matches)\"` into a true hit list and an explicit error respectively. No caller passes a file\npath today expecting `\"(no matches)\"` — that value is indistinguishable from the honest-absence\nanswer, so nothing can be relying on it.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `search_files(query, path)` where `path` names an existing FILE that contains `query` returns\n      the `file:line: text` hit(s), not `\"(no matches)\"`\n- [ ] `search_files(query, path)` where `path` names an existing FILE that does NOT contain `query`\n      still returns `\"(no matches)\"` (honest absence is preserved)\n- [ ] `search_files(query, path)` where `path` names a DIRECTORY is unchanged — same hits, same\n      ordering, same caps (regression pin; every existing gate depends on it)\n- [ ] `search_files(query, path)` where `path` does not exist returns an explicit `Error: ...`,\n      never `\"(no matches)\"`\n- [ ] a **gitignored** file named explicitly as `path`, which contains `query`, returns its hits —\n      the discovery filter must not hide a file the caller named (pinned with a git fixture that\n      asserts the filter is genuinely armed before trusting the result)\n- [ ] the repo-root confinement and deny-list of `_safe_path` still apply to a file path — a file\n      outside the root or on the deny-list is refused exactly as a directory is\n- [ ] `search_files`'s docstring states that `path` may name a file (scanned as one file) or a\n      directory (walked), and that an unsearchable path returns `Error: ...` — the docstring is the\n      model-facing tool contract Pydantic AI passes to the agent (`pai_tools.py:46`)\n- [ ] the RED test is shown failing for the right reason before the fix exists, and passing after\n- [ ] the full CI selection (`pytest -m \"not integration and not external\"`) stays green\n\n## Testing\n\n    pytest tests/unit/test_pai_tools_search_files_bf31.py -q\n    pytest -m \"not integration and not external\" -n 4 --dist worksteal -q"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785931810304686001,"uuid":"d5cee3a5-377b-42f4-a188-0fe89aab923e"}